"""Detailed mathematical arguments for the strongly convex developments.

The web overviews and paper exports use these arguments. References identify
the precise statements supporting each application.
"""


def part(identifier, title, body, file, name):
    return dict(id=identifier, title=title, body=body,
                references=[dict(file=file, name=name)])


ULA_PROOF = [
part('quadratic-models', 'Gradient-step contraction from strong convexity and smoothness', r'''
<p>Let \(g=\nabla U\). Differentiability, strong convexity, and gradient Lipschitzness imply</p>\[U(x)+\langle g(x),y-x\rangle+\frac m2\|y-x\|^2
\le U(y)\le U(x)+\langle g(x),y-x\rangle+\frac L2\|y-x\|^2.\]<p>Subtracting \(m\|x\|^2/2\) gives a convex differentiable function, whose supporting inequality proves the lower bound. For the upper bound, integrate the gradient along the segment from \(x\) to \(y\) and bound its remainder by \(L\int_0^1t\,dt\,\|y-x\|^2\). Neither step requires a Hessian.</p><p>The shifted function \(U-m\|\cdot\|^2/2\) has upper quadratic coefficient \(L-m\). Its cocoercive inequality, expressed with \(d=x-y\) and \(v=g(x)-g(y)\), gives</p>\[mL\|d\|^2+\|v\|^2\le(m+L)\langle v,d\rangle.\]<p>Completion of a square yields</p>\[\left\|v-\frac{m+L}{2}d\right\|\le\frac{L-m}{2}\|d\|.\]<p>The triangle inequality therefore bounds \(\|d-hv\|\) by</p>\[\left(\left|1-\frac{m+L}{2}h\right|+\frac{L-m}{2}h\right)\|d\|.\]<p>For \(0\le h\le2/(m+L)\) this coefficient equals \(1-mh\); for \(2/(m+L)\le h\le2/L\) it equals \(Lh-1\). At \(m=L\) the radius in the preceding norm inequality is zero, so the same argument remains valid without division by \(L-m\).</p>
''', 'UnadjustedLangevin/SharpContraction.lean', 'strong_smooth_gradient_step_contract_max'),
part('gibbs-moments', 'Gibbs normalization and score moments', r'''
<p>Strong convexity at a fixed \(x_0\) bounds \(U(x)\) below by a positive quadratic plus an affine function. Completing the square bounds \(e^{-U(x)}\), and its product with a quadratic polynomial in \(x\), by integrable translated Gaussians. Strict positivity then gives a positive finite partition and a finite Gibbs second moment (see <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Gibbs.lean" data-report-declaration="isProbabilityMeasure_gibbsMeasure"></cite> and <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Gibbs.lean" data-report-declaration="integrable_norm_sq_gibbsMeasure"></cite>).</p><p>The gradient second moment follows from the smooth upper model. For every \(v\),</p>\[U(x-v)\le U(x)-\langle g(x),v\rangle+\frac L2\|v\|^2.\]<p>Rearrange, exponentiate, and integrate. Translation invariance of volume makes the translated partition equal to the original one, giving</p>\[\int e^{\langle g(x),v\rangle}\,\pi(dx)\le e^{L\|v\|^2/2}.\]<p>For each unit coordinate direction, apply this inequality to both signs of a scalar multiple. Differentiation of the resulting moment-generating bound at zero gives zero directional mean and second moment at most \(L\). Summing over an orthonormal basis gives</p>\[\int\|g(x)\|^2\,\pi(dx)\le Ld.\]<p>Thus gradient integrability and its quantitative bound follow from the first-derivative hypotheses. They justify the reference drift integrals used in the local-error estimate.</p>
''', 'UnadjustedLangevin/GibbsScore.lean', 'integral_norm_sq_gradient_gibbs_le_of_strongConvex'),
part('stationary-reference', 'Existence and stationarity of the Langevin comparison process', r'''
<p>Let \(B_t\) be a centered Gaussian process with independent increments, covariance \(\mathbb E[B_sB_t^{\mathsf T}]=\min(s,t)I_d\), and mean-square continuity, independent of \(Y_0\sim\pi\). In the Hilbert space of square-integrable vectors solve</p>\[Y_t=Y_0-\int_0^t g(Y_s)\,ds+\sqrt2 B_t.\]<p>The gradient composition is \(L\)-Lipschitz. Finite-horizon Picard iteration gives a continuous integral solution, and Grönwall applied to the difference of two solutions gives uniqueness.</p><p>To prove stationarity, initialize the Metropolis-adjusted Euler transition in \(\pi\). Detailed balance preserves \(\pi\). At mesh \(\delta\), its expected rejection-weighted displacement is at most \(C\delta^{3/2}\) with finite \(C\) uniform for sufficiently small \(\delta\). Couple adjusted and unadjusted transitions with common proposals. Nonexpansiveness of those proposals over \(n\) steps gives mean endpoint separation at most \(nC\delta^{3/2}\).</p><p>Fix \(T>0\) and use \(\delta=T/n\). Brownian mesh increments realize the unadjusted chain, and the bound becomes</p>\[\mathbb E\|X_n^{\mathrm{Euler}}-X_n^{\mathrm{adjusted}}\|
\le TC\sqrt\delta\longrightarrow0.\]<p>The Euler approximation with the same initial input converges to \(Y_T\) in mean square. For frequency \(v\), the characteristic test \(x\mapsto e^{i\langle v,x\rangle}\) is bounded and has Lipschitz constant \(\|v\|\). Both comparisons therefore give convergence of its expectations. Since each adjusted endpoint has law \(\pi\), the characteristic functions of \(Y_T\) and \(\pi\) coincide. Uniqueness of characteristic functions gives \(\operatorname{Law}(Y_T)=\pi\), as stated in <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/GibbsStationarity.lean" data-report-declaration="lp_langevin_gibbs_stationary"></cite>. For \(T=0\) this is the initialization property.</p><p>Stationarity and the score moment consequently give \(\|g(Y_t)\|_{L^2}\le\sqrt{Ld}\) at every time. The Brownian construction also gives independent future increments for the one-step coupling.</p>
''', 'UnadjustedLangevin/GibbsStationarity.lean', 'lp_langevin_gibbs_stationary'),
part('local-defect', 'The integrated local Euler error', r'''
<p>On an interval of length \(h\), initialize the stationary process at \(Y_0\) and define</p>\[R_h=\int_0^h\bigl(g(Y_s)-g(Y_0)\bigr)\,ds.\]<p>The integral equation gives \(Y_h=Y_0-hg(Y_0)+\sqrt2B_h-R_h\). Minkowski and stationarity imply</p>\[\|Y_s-Y_0\|_{L^2}\le s\sqrt{Ld}+\sqrt{2ds}.\]<p>The score moment <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/GibbsScore.lean" data-report-declaration="integral_norm_sq_gradient_gibbs_le_of_strongConvex"></cite> bounds the drift integral by \(\int_0^s\sqrt{Ld}\,du\), and the Brownian moment is \(\|B_s\|_{L^2}=\sqrt{ds}\). Gradient Lipschitzness then bounds the integrand of \(R_h\). Hence</p>\[\begin{aligned}
\|R_h\|_{L^2}
&\le L\int_0^h\bigl(s\sqrt{Ld}+\sqrt{2ds}\bigr)\,ds\\
&=L\left(\frac{h^2}{2}\sqrt{Ld}+\frac23h^{3/2}\sqrt{2d}\right).
\end{aligned}\]<p>Under \(Lh\le2\), division by \(Lh\sqrt{hd}\) leaves coefficient at most \(\sqrt2/2+2\sqrt2/3=7\sqrt2/6\le33/20\), so</p>\[\|R_h\|_{L^2}\le\frac{33}{20}Lh\sqrt{hd}.\]<p>The local factor \(h^{3/2}\) divided by the contraction gap of order \(h\) gives a global error of order \(\sqrt h\). This calculation determines the numerical coefficient without an additional local-consistency hypothesis.</p>
''', 'UnadjustedLangevin/LocalError.lean', 'localDefect_dalalyan_bound'),
part('synchronous-coupling', 'The synchronous Euler–Langevin comparison', r'''
<p>Choose a finite-cost coupling with marginals \(X_0\sim\mu_0\) and \(Y_0\sim\pi\). Extend it by Brownian noise independent of the initial pair, and use \((B_{(k+1)h}-B_{kh})/\sqrt h\) as the Euler innovations. Independence makes the numerical marginal \(\mu_k\) independent of the chosen initial coupling.</p><p>Subtract the reference equation for \(Y\) from the Euler update. The common noise cancels; with \(T_h(x)=x-hg(x)\),</p>\[X_{k+1}-Y_{(k+1)h}=T_h(X_k)-T_h(Y_{kh})+R_{k,h}.\]<p>Apply deterministic contraction <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/SharpContraction.lean" data-report-declaration="strong_smooth_gradient_step_contract_max"></cite> to the first difference and the stationary local estimate <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LocalError.lean" data-report-declaration="localDefect_dalalyan_bound"></cite> on each interval. For \(a_k=\|X_k-Y_{kh}\|_{L^2}\) this gives</p>\[a_{k+1}\le q a_k+b,\qquad b=\frac{33}{20}Lh\sqrt{hd}.\]<p>The relevant ratio is \(q=1-mh\) or \(q=Lh-1\), with \(0\le q&lt;1\) in its stated regime. The pair \((X_k,Y_{kh})\) has marginals \(\mu_k\) and \(\pi\), so its root mean square separation bounds their transport distance. Lipschitz growth and Gaussian second moments preserve square integrability at every finite step.</p>
''', 'UnadjustedLangevin/HilbertDalalyan.lean', 'dalalyan_small_step_of_integral_reference'),
part('geometric-sum', 'The geometric sum and initial transport infimum', r'''
<p>Induction over \(k\) gives</p>\[a_k\le q^ka_0+b\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}q^j
=q^ka_0+\frac{b(1-q^k)}{1-q}
\le q^ka_0+\frac b{1-q}.\]<p>Its denominator is positive under \(q&lt;1\). Since the output marginals are the same for every finite-cost initial coupling, taking their initial-cost infimum replaces \(a_0\) by \(W_2(\mu_0,\pi)\). Existence of an optimal coupling is unnecessary.</p><p>In the first step regime \(1-q=mh\) gives \(b/(1-q)=(33/20)(L/m)\sqrt{hd}\). In the second, \(1-q=2-Lh\) gives \(b/(1-q)=(33/20)Lh\sqrt{hd}/(2-Lh)\). The two expressions agree at \(h=2/(m+L)\). For fixed positive \(h\) only the initial contribution tends to zero as \(k\) increases. The remaining bound is reduced by reducing \(h\), as the displayed discretization term specifies.</p>
''', 'UnadjustedLangevin/Recurrence.lean', 'affine_recurrence_exact'),
]


IPLA_PROOF = [
part('scaled-geometry', 'The scaled particle geometry', r'''
<p>For \(N\ge1\) use coordinates \(z=(\theta,x^1/\sqrt N,\ldots,x^N/\sqrt N)\) with squared norm \(\|\theta\|^2+N^{-1}\sum_i\|x^i\|^2\). The pair map \(J_i z=(\theta,x^i)\) satisfies</p>\[\sum_{i=1}^N\|J_i(z-z')\|^2=N\|z-z'\|^2.\]<p>Summing the pair strong-convexity inequalities gives quadratic correction \(Nm\|z-z'\|^2/2\). Thus \(V_N=\sum_iU_y\circ J_i\) has curvature \(Nm\). For the gradient constant \(NL\), apply the chain rule through \(J_i\), test a gradient difference in an arbitrary direction, and apply Cauchy–Schwarz over particles. The same squared-norm identity bounds both factors, yielding the stated constant.</p><p>The dimension is \(D=d_\theta+Nd_x\). The argument permits unequal parameter and latent dimensions, and zero-dimensional blocks contribute zero to the sums.</p>
''', 'IPLA/Geometry.lean', 'potential_gradient_lipschitz'),
part('algorithm-law', 'The particle recursion as a Gaussian Euler update', r'''
<p>The chain rule in the parameter and latent blocks gives</p>\[\nabla_\theta V_N(z)=\sum_i\nabla_\theta U_y(\theta,x^i),\qquad
\nabla_{z_i}V_N(z)=\sqrt N\nabla_xU_y(\theta,x^i).\]<p>Use Gaussian Euler for \(V_N\) at step \(s=\gamma/N\). The parameter drift is the average pair gradient and its Gaussian coefficient is \(\sqrt{2\gamma/N}\). Multiplying a scaled latent update by \(\sqrt N\) gives drift coefficient \(\gamma\) and noise coefficient \(\sqrt{2\gamma}\). These are the physical IPLA updates.</p><p>The independent next innovation is independent of the current state, so its conditional update is the Gaussian Euler transition. Iterating this transition gives the particle laws. The restriction \(s\le2/(Nm+NL)\) is equivalent to \(\gamma\le2/(m+L)\) under positive particle count.</p>
''', 'IPLA/Geometry.lean', 'scaledUpdate_particle'),
part('parameter-target', 'Latent integration and marginal strong convexity', r'''
<p>The normalization and second-moment theorems <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Gibbs.lean" data-report-declaration="isProbabilityMeasure_gibbsMeasure"></cite> and <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Gibbs.lean" data-report-declaration="integrable_norm_sq_gibbsMeasure"></cite> apply to the strongly convex summed potential. Change the scaled latent blocks back to physical vectors. Its volume factor is independent of \(\theta\) and cancels on normalization. Tonelli gives</p>\[\int\exp\!\left(-\sum_iU_y(\theta,x^i)\right)\prod_i dx^i
=\prod_i\int e^{-U_y(\theta,x)}dx=\ell_y(\theta)^N.\]<p>The nonnegative product integral factors before finiteness is used. Gaussian domination and positivity then make \(F(\theta)=-\log\ell_y(\theta)\) an ordinary real marginal potential.</p><p>To prove curvature of \(F\), subtract the parameter quadratic corresponding to \(m\) from \(U_y\). The remaining exponential is jointly log-concave. The finite-dimensional Prékopa–Leindler theorem preserves log-concavity under latent integration, and restoration of the quadratic gives the claimed strong convexity. Continuity and coercivity give a minimizer, whose uniqueness follows from strict convexity. It is the marginal-likelihood maximizer \(\theta_\star\).</p>
''', 'IPLA/StrongLogConcavity.lean', 'marginalPotential_strongConvex'),
part('equilibrium-radius', 'Equilibrium fluctuations about the marginal maximizer', r'''
<p>The parameter marginal potential \(NF\) has curvature \(Nm\) and minimizer \(\theta_\star\). The continuous strongly convex concentration theorem <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/GibbsConcentration.lean" data-report-declaration="integral_sq_dist_gibbs_le_of_continuous_strongConvex_mode"></cite> gives</p>\[\int\|\theta-\theta_\star\|^2\,\Pi_N(dz)\le\frac{2d_\theta}{Nm}.\]<p>Its nonnegative square root is \(r_N=\sqrt{2d_\theta/(Nm)}\). This is the radius about the marginal mode; minimizing over latent coordinates instead of integrating them would generally give a different point. Although the dynamics have dimension \(D\), only parameter dimension appears in this concentration estimate. Under \(d_\theta=0\) the projection is constant and the radius vanishes. A separate dilation argument establishes the improved factor used in later proximal and kinetic estimates.</p>
''', 'IPLA/Concentration.lean', 'gibbsTarget_parameter_concentration'),
part('transport-projection', 'The parameter projection of a transport coupling', r'''
<p>Define \(Pz=\theta\). Orthogonality of the state blocks gives \(\|P(z-z')\|\le\|z-z'\|\). For a coupling \((Z,Z_*)\) of \(\mu_k\) and \(\Pi_N\), Minkowski yields</p>\[\begin{aligned}
\|PZ-\theta_\star\|_{L^2}
&\le\|P(Z-Z_*)\|_{L^2}+\|PZ_*-\theta_\star\|_{L^2}\\
&\le\|Z-Z_*\|_{L^2}+r_N.
\end{aligned}\]<p>Taking the finite-cost coupling infimum, as in <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Projection.lean" data-report-declaration="projectedRMSReal_le_wasserstein2Real_add"></cite>, gives \(\|\theta_k-\theta_\star\|_{L^2}\le W_2(\mu_k,\Pi_N)+r_N\). Finite second moments of both laws justify these real quantities. No particle independence at equilibrium or finite time is needed for this projection argument.</p>
''', 'IPLA/Geometry.lean', 'norm_parameterCLM_apply_le'),
part('particle-scaling', 'The particle-scaled transport and parameter bound', r'''
<p>Use curvature \(Nm\), gradient constant \(NL\), dimension \(D=d_\theta+Nd_x\), and step \(s=\gamma/N\) in the Gaussian Euler estimate <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/CanonicalULA.lean" data-report-declaration="dalalyan"></cite>. The contraction becomes \(1-(Nm)s=1-m\gamma\) and the dimension term satisfies</p>\[\frac{NL}{Nm}\sqrt{sD}
=\frac Lm\sqrt{\gamma\left(d_x+\frac{d_\theta}{N}\right)}.\]<p>Adding the equilibrium radius by the preceding projection inequality proves the parameter estimate. The powers of \(N\) are determined by the coordinate and time scalings. Parameter noise and equilibrium spread decrease with \(N\), while the latent discretization contribution has order \(\sqrt{\gamma d_x}\).</p><p>Thus \(N\) controls equilibrium concentration, \(\gamma\) controls the time-discretization error, and \(k\) controls the remaining initial error. At a fixed physical step particle count alone does not remove the latent numerical error. Unequal dimensions are included by the same identities.</p>
''', 'IPLA/Theorem.lean', 'theorem1'),
]


PROXIMAL_PROOF = [
part('proximal-calculus', 'Proximal optimality and differentiability of the envelope', r'''
<p>For \(\eta&gt;0\), add quadratic penalty \(\|p-x\|^2/(2\eta)\) to the finite convex function \(g_2(p)\). Under the stated lower bound the objective is coercive and strictly convex, so it has unique minimizer \(P_\eta(x)\). Its residual support at two inputs and subgradient monotonicity give</p>\[\|P_\eta(x)-P_\eta(y)\|^2\le
\langle P_\eta(x)-P_\eta(y),x-y\rangle.\]<p>Expansion of residual differences also makes \(I-P_\eta\) nonexpansive. Compare the envelope objective at \(x+u\) using its minimizer at \(x\), and conversely. The resulting two-sided quadratic remainder proves</p>\[\nabla g_2^\eta(x)=\frac{x-P_\eta(x)}\eta.\]<p>Residual nonexpansiveness gives gradient constant \(\eta^{-1}\). Convexity of the envelope means \(V_\eta=g_1+g_2^\eta\) retains curvature \(m\) and has gradient constant \(L_\eta=L_1+\eta^{-1}\). These established properties meet the Gaussian Euler transport theorem <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/CanonicalULA.lean" data-report-declaration="dalalyan"></cite>.</p>
''', 'ProximalLangevin/Moreau.lean', 'gradient_moreau'),
part('finite-smoothing-gap', 'The finite Moreau potential-gap estimate', r'''
<p>Competitor \(p=x\) in the minimizing objective gives \(g_2^\eta(x)\le g_2(x)\). Conversely the value Lipschitz bound gives, for all \(p\),</p>\[g_2(p)+\frac{\|p-x\|^2}{2\eta}
\ge g_2(x)-K\|p-x\|+\frac{\|p-x\|^2}{2\eta}
\ge g_2(x)-\frac{\eta K^2}{2}.\]<p>The last step completes a scalar square in \(\|p-x\|\). Evaluation at the proximal minimizer therefore gives</p>\[V_\eta\le U\le V_\eta+\delta_\eta,\qquad\delta_\eta=\eta K^2/2.\]<p>Exponentiation reverses potential order and latent integration preserves the resulting nonnegative-function order. Taking negative logarithms of the positive finite marginal integrals gives, for \(F_U,F_\eta\), the relation \(F_\eta\le F_U\le F_\eta+\delta_\eta\). The function \(U=g_1+g_2\) is continuous because \(g_1\) is differentiable and \(g_2\) is Lipschitz. The comparison gives Gaussian domination locally uniform in the parameter; dominated convergence therefore proves continuity of the original marginal. Its decay as the parameter norm tends to infinity gives existence of a marginal maximizer.</p>
''', 'ProximalLangevin/Moreau.lean', 'moreau_gap_of_lipschitz'),
part('center-stability', 'Marginal-potential comparison and center displacement', r'''
<p>Let \(\theta_U\) and \(\theta_\eta\) minimize \(F_U\) and \(F_\eta\). Marginal strong convexity <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/StrongLogConcavity.lean" data-report-declaration="marginalPotential_strongConvex"></cite> makes \(F_\eta\) \(m\)-strongly convex. Quadratic growth, the uniform gap, and minimality of \(\theta_U\) imply</p>\[\begin{aligned}
\frac m2\|\theta_U-\theta_\eta\|^2
&\le F_\eta(\theta_U)-F_\eta(\theta_\eta)\\
&\le F_U(\theta_U)-F_\eta(\theta_\eta)\\
&\le F_U(\theta_\eta)-F_\eta(\theta_\eta)
\le\delta_\eta.
\end{aligned}\]<p>Taking square roots yields \(\|\theta_U-\theta_\eta\|\le K\sqrt{\eta/m}\) for every positive \(\eta\). The argument does not differentiate the original nonsmooth potential. Its order is \(\sqrt\eta\); the proved result therefore does not support replacing this term by a linear smoothing rate.</p>
''', 'ProximalLangevin/Perturbation.lean', 'marginal_center_distance'),
part('smoothed-particle-bound', 'The MYIPLA bound about the original marginal maximizer', r'''
<p>MYIPLA equals IPLA for \(V_\eta\). The summed-potential statements <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Geometry.lean" data-report-declaration="potential_strongConvex"></cite> and <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Geometry.lean" data-report-declaration="potential_gradient_lipschitz"></cite> give curvature \(Nm\), gradient constant \(NL_\eta\), and dimension \(D=d_\theta+Nd_x\). Step \(\gamma/N\) gives contraction \(1-m\gamma\). Apply Gaussian Euler transport <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/CanonicalULA.lean" data-report-declaration="dalalyan"></cite> and parameter projection <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Projection.lean" data-report-declaration="projectedRMSReal_le_wasserstein2Real_add"></cite>.</p><p>The sharp marginal estimate <cite data-report-ref="ProximalLangevin/SharpIPLA.lean" data-report-declaration="sharp_gibbsTarget_parameter_concentration"></cite> gives radius \(\sqrt{d_\theta/(Nm)}\). Minkowski adds the center displacement. The four contributions are</p>\[K\sqrt{\eta/m},\quad\sqrt{\frac{d_\theta}{Nm}},\quad
(1-m\gamma)^nW_2(\rho_0,\Pi_{N,\eta}),\quad
\frac{33}{20}\frac{L_\eta}{m}\sqrt{\gamma\left(d_x+\frac{d_\theta}{N}\right)}.\]<p>A smaller \(\eta\) reduces the first contribution while increasing \(L_\eta\) and restricting the admissible step. Thus smoothing and discretization choices must satisfy the displayed joint conditions. The estimate is for the stated Gaussian particle law.</p>
''', 'ProximalLangevin/Theorem.lean', 'moreau_myipla_parameter_rms_original'),
part('splitting-energy', 'The equal-scale PIPGLA transport estimate', r'''
<p>Take \(\lambda=\gamma\). Apply the smooth noisy forward update and then the pairwise proximal maps, averaging their parameter outputs. Couple its noise with the stationary Langevin reference for \(V_\gamma\) supplied by <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/GibbsStationarity.lean" data-report-declaration="lp_langevin_gibbs_stationary"></cite>. Keep the proximal support inner products in the squared-error expansion until their energy terms are combined.</p><p>Put \(s=\gamma/N\), \(a=Nm\), \(b=NL_1\), and \(c=NK\). Drift and noise moment bounds are \(G=\sqrt{2b^2D/a+2c^2}+c\) and \(\sigma=\sqrt{2D}\). The local increment is bounded by \(Gs+\sigma\sqrt s\). Squaring with \((u+v)^2\le2u^2+2v^2\) and using \(s\le1/a\) bounds the higher powers by the coefficient</p>\[C_N=\frac{4b^2}{a^2}\left(\frac{G^2}{a}+\sigma^2\right)+\frac2a(Gc+c^2).\]<p>For gap \(\delta=N\gamma K^2/2=sc^2/2\), the one-step forcing is</p>\[\mathcal F=(1+(as)^{-1})[bs(Gs+\sigma\sqrt s)]^2+2s\delta+2s^2Gc+s^2c^2.\]<p>Weighted Young controls the smooth defect. Convex support, stationarity, and zero expected pairings with future Brownian increments give \(2s\delta+2s^2Gc\) for the energy cross term. The remaining term is the proximal quadratic remainder. Under \(s\le1/a\) these estimates imply \(\mathcal F\le as^2C_N\). The recurrence coefficient is \(1-a s=1-m\gamma\), so the initial coupling infimum and geometric sum give</p>\[W_2(\rho_n,\Pi_{N,\gamma})^2
\le(1-m\gamma)^nW_2(\rho_0,\Pi_{N,\gamma})^2+\frac\gamma N C_N.\]<p>Projection adds the sharp equilibrium radius to the square root of this transport bound, and the center comparison adds \(K\sqrt{\gamma/m}\). Since the recurrence controls squared transport, its transient factor occurs inside the final square root.</p>
''', 'ProximalLangevin/SplittingRate.lean', 'splittingCouplingForcing_le'),
part('proximal-parameter', 'PIPGLA comparison at two positive proximal scales', r'''
<p>For a convex \(K\)-Lipschitz penalty, proximal optimality and nonexpansiveness at a shifted input give</p>\[\|P_\lambda(x)-P_\eta(x)\|\le K|\lambda-\eta|.\]<p>Apply this estimate to every pair. Jensen for the parameter average and the exact latent scaling give the same full-state bound \(K|\lambda-\eta|\) per step. Gradient-step contraction <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/SharpContraction.lean" data-report-declaration="strong_smooth_gradient_step_contract"></cite> has factor \(q=1-m\gamma\), and proximal nonexpansiveness preserves it when both chains use the same innovation.</p><p>From common initialization, geometric summation bounds their difference by \(K|\lambda-\eta|/(1-q)=K|\lambda-\eta|/(m\gamma)\) uniformly over finite iteration counts. Set \(\eta=\gamma\) and combine this comparison with the equal-scale bound. Minkowski adds the same constant to the parameter root mean square error.</p>
''', 'ProximalLangevin/SplittingTheorem.lean', 'pipgla_parameter_rms_prox_comparison'),
part('noise-removal', 'The finite-particle MYPGD–MYIPLA comparison', r'''
<p>Extend any finite-cost coupling of the current MYPGD and MYIPLA laws by an independent standard Gaussian vector. Both updates use its latent component; only MYIPLA uses its parameter component. That omitted component is centered and has squared \(L^2\) norm \(2\gamma d_\theta/N\). Independence makes its mixed expectation with the deterministic discrepancy zero.</p><p>The smooth contraction theorem <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/SharpContraction.lean" data-report-declaration="strong_smooth_gradient_step_contract"></cite> gives factor \(1-m\gamma\). The constructed output coupling thus has squared cost at most \((1-m\gamma)^2\) times the input squared cost plus the omitted variance. Taking the input coupling infimum, for \(E_n=W_2(\rho_n^{\mathrm{PGD}},\rho_n^{\mathrm{MYIPLA}})^2\), gives</p>\[E_{n+1}\le(1-m\gamma)^2E_n+\frac{2\gamma d_\theta}{N},\qquad E_0=0.\]<p>The initial law is common, and finite moments justify all transport squares. Step admissibility gives \(0\le m\gamma\le1\) and therefore \(1-(1-m\gamma)^2=m\gamma(2-m\gamma)\ge m\gamma\). The geometric sum gives \(E_n\le2d_\theta/(Nm)\). Parameter contraction <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Geometry.lean" data-report-declaration="norm_parameterCLM_apply_le"></cite> and projected root mean square inequality <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Projection.lean" data-report-declaration="projectedRMSReal_le_wasserstein2Real_add"></cite> consequently add \(\sqrt{2d_\theta/(Nm)}\) to the MYIPLA parameter bound.</p><p>The comparison radius is a finite-particle upper bound and remains for finite \(N\). This result does not assert the stronger mean-field metric estimate; mean-field integral constructions and their particle comparisons are established separately.</p>
''', 'ProximalLangevin/MYPGDTheorem.lean', 'mypgd_myipla_actual_transport_sq'),
]

# Authored proof correspondence for the sequential mathematical argument.
_ARGUMENT_LEAN = {'quadratic-models': '<p><code>strong_smooth_gradient_step_contract_max</code> takes <code>hdiff</code>, <code>hsc</code> and <code>hLip</code> for differentiability, strong convexity and the gradient Lipschitz bound. Its <code>hmL</code>, <code>hpos</code> and <code>hh</code> require the displayed order of constants, a positive sum and a nonnegative step. The proof splits at <code>hs</code>. In the first case it calls <code>strong_smooth_gradient_step_contract</code>; in the other it calls <code>strong_smooth_gradient_step_contract_large</code>. The first coefficient is bounded by the absolute value in the maximum. In the second case <code>hfactor</code> bounds the coefficient by the other absolute value. Multiplication preserves these comparisons because the distance is nonnegative. The theorem itself permits any nonnegative step; a coefficient below one requires the stability conditions used later.</p>', 'gibbs-moments': '<p><code>integral_norm_sq_gradient_gibbs_le_of_strongConvex</code> first obtains a finite partition function from <code>gibbsPartition_ne_top</code>, using positive curvature <code>hm</code>, differentiability <code>hdiff</code> and strong convexity <code>hsc</code>. It then applies <code>integral_norm_sq_gradient_gibbs_le</code> with <code>hLip</code>. The latter is the score-moment theorem under a finite partition; the earlier declarations in this module establish its directional exponential-moment bound and pass to coordinate second moments. <code>Module.finrank</code> is the finite vector-space dimension, so the conclusion is precisely the sum of the coordinate bounds.</p>', 'stationary-reference': '<p>In <code>lp_langevin_gibbs_stationary</code>, <code>hYe</code> is the integral equation in the Hilbert space <code>Lp</code> at exponent two, <code>hinitial</code> is the initial Gibbs law, and <code>hscalar</code> and <code>hind</code> provide the stated Gaussian independence. <code>hTzero</code> separates the initial-time identity. For positive time, <code>step</code> is a sufficiently fine decreasing mesh; <code>hstationary</code> proves invariance of every adjusted step. <code>Xn</code> is the Euler endpoint and <code>ρ</code> its coupling with the adjusted chain. <code>hX</code> proves convergence to the integral solution, <code>hfst</code> and <code>hsnd</code> identify both marginals, and <code>hint</code> justifies the coupling-distance integral. <code>hbound</code> and <code>hcost</code> make that distance tend to zero. Finally <code>measure_map_Lp_eq_of_coupling_cost_tendsto</code> identifies the limiting law. Here <code>P.map</code> denotes the distribution of a random variable under the probability measure.</p>', 'local-defect': '<p><code>localDefect_dalalyan_bound</code> is a deterministic Hilbert-space estimate, so it also applies to the Hilbert space of square-integrable random vectors. Its <code>hG</code> and <code>hB</code> are the drift and forcing norm bounds, while <code>heq</code> is the integral equation. The proof obtains <code>hsmall</code> by squaring two nonnegative expressions and using <code>hstep</code>. It rewrites the square-root factors in <code>hnoise</code>, then invokes <code>localDefect_bound</code> for the explicit time integral. The final two inequalities collect the coefficient and use <code>dalalyan_constant_bound</code>. The assumptions <code>hh</code> and <code>hd</code> allow zero time or zero dimension without division.</p>', 'synchronous-coupling': '<p><code>dalalyan_small_step_of_integral_reference</code> receives <code>ref</code>, an <code>L2Reference</code> whose fields contain the segment equations, target laws and moment bounds. <code>hproduct</code>, <code>hmh</code> and <code>hstep</code> derive the scalar step consequences. These give the nonnegative contraction factor <code>hq</code> and its strict upper bound <code>hq1</code>. The invocation of <code>l2_reference_bound</code> combines the integral local defect with <code>lpDrift_gradient_contract</code>. <code>hid</code> cancels the positive step from the forcing divided by the contraction gap, producing the displayed constant. This theorem is conditional on its reference; the preceding stationarity and product-construction results supply that reference for the final Gaussian algorithm.</p>', 'geometric-sum': '<p><code>affine_recurrence_exact</code> takes the recurrence as <code>hstep</code>, the nonnegative multiplier as <code>hq</code> and its strict upper bound as <code>hq1</code>. It applies <code>affine_recurrence_sum</code>, whose induction retains the finite geometric sum. The subsequent <code>geom_sum_eq</code> rewrite requires that the multiplier differ from one, supplied by <code>ne_of_lt hq1</code>. The two sign rewrites convert the denominator and numerator to their positive-gap form. No nonnegativity of the forcing or the initial value is required for this exact finite-sum estimate; the later replacement of the numerator by one uses the nonnegative forcing present here.</p>', 'scaled-geometry': '<p><code>potential_gradient_lipschitz</code> proves the bound through <code>LipschitzWith.of_dist_le_mul</code>. Its local claim <code>hi</code> pairs the gradient difference with an arbitrary direction. <code>inner_gradient_potential</code> rewrites that pairing as a sum over physical pairs; <code>joint_sub</code> and <code>hLip.norm_sub_le</code> bound each summand. <code>sum_joint_norm_mul</code> is the finite Cauchy–Schwarz estimate using the replicated-pair squared-norm identity. The proof then substitutes the gradient difference itself for the direction. If its norm is zero, <code>hz</code> closes the estimate directly; otherwise positivity permits cancellation of that norm. The differentiability assumption <code>hU</code> is used in the chain-rule pairing formula.</p>', 'algorithm-law': '<p><code>scaledUpdate_particle</code> assumes <code>hN</code> so the particle count is nonzero. <code>gradient_potential_particle hU</code> supplies the exact latent derivative of the summed potential. The proof first establishes <code>hn</code>, the identity for the rescaled noise coefficient. After expanding <code>scaledUpdate</code> coordinatewise, <code>hd</code> cancels the two square-root particle factors in the drift. Rewriting by these two identities yields the physical-particle update. This algebraic identity itself permits any real step; Gaussian transition and probability results impose a positive step when they use it.</p>', 'parameter-target': '<p><code>marginalPotential_strongConvex</code> constructs the defining convexity inequality at arbitrary nonnegative weights <code>a</code>, <code>b</code> summing to one. <code>hf</code>, <code>hg</code> and <code>hh</code> provide Gaussian domination of the three latent weights. <code>hpoint</code> follows from <code>hsc.2</code> after <code>pair_affine</code> and <code>pair_sub</code>; the nonnegative latent squared distance is discarded while the parameter curvature term <code>c</code> is retained. <code>Prekopa.prekopa_leindler</code> integrates this pointwise inequality. <code>integral_latentWeight</code> identifies the marginal integrals, <code>hz</code> proves strict positivity, and <code>Real.log_le_log</code> turns the integrated inequality into strong convexity of the negative logarithm.</p>', 'equilibrium-radius': '<p><code>gibbsTarget_parameter_concentration</code> obtains the probability instance and square integrability from the strong-convex Gibbs results. <code>continuousLinearMap_comp</code> transports square integrability through the parameter projection, and subtraction of <code>memLp_const θStar</code> centers it. <code>projectedRMS_le_of_secondMoment</code> reduces the desired root bound to the second-moment estimate. <code>Real.sq_sqrt</code> cancels the square root using nonnegativity, and <code>gibbsTarget_parameter_secondMoment_le</code> supplies that estimate. The premise <code>hmax</code> identifies the marginal maximizer; it is not a joint minimizer hypothesis.</p>', 'transport-projection': '<p><code>norm_parameterCLM_apply_le</code> rewrites both squared norms with <code>EuclideanSpace.real_norm_sq_eq</code>. <code>Fintype.sum_sum_type</code> separates parameter coordinates from latent coordinates; the latter sum is nonnegative term by term by <code>sq_nonneg</code>. The local inequality <code>hs</code> therefore compares the squared norms. Nonnegativity of both norms permits the final passage to their unsquared comparison. The transport consequence uses this pointwise contraction inside any coupling and then the coupling infimum, as in <code>projectedRMSReal_le_wasserstein2Real_add</code> in the cited projection module.</p>', 'particle-scaling': '<p><code>theorem1</code> receives the differentiable, strongly convex, smooth pair potential together with positive curvature, positive step, Gaussian independence and the marginal-maximizer premise. It splits on <code>hindex</code>, which records whether the full state has any coordinate. In the nonempty case <code>nonempty_dimension_bound</code> assembles scaled Gaussian transport and parameter concentration. In the empty case, <code>hθ</code> and <code>hx</code> force both dimensions to zero, so the parameter error and every dimensional contribution vanish. The initial state is supplied as an <code>Lp</code> vector of exponent two; the Gaussian assumptions identify its algorithmic laws with the transition iterates.</p>', 'proximal-calculus': '<p><code>gradient_moreau</code> identifies the actual gradient by applying the uniqueness property <code>.gradient</code> to <code>moreau_hasGradientAt</code>. The underlying derivative proof has already obtained the two-sided remainder from the same minimizing-point comparisons described above. The section assumptions <code>hf</code>, <code>hb</code> and <code>hlam</code> are convexity, a global lower bound and a positive proximal scale. <code>residual</code> is the scaled displacement from the proximal minimizer, so the conclusion is the displayed envelope-gradient formula. Smoothness follows in the subsequent declarations by combining that identity with nonexpansiveness of the residual map.</p>', 'finite-smoothing-gap': '<p><code>moreau_gap_of_lipschitz</code> proves both halves of the potential gap. For the lower half it invokes <code>moreau_le</code>, the competitor-at-the-input inequality. For the upper half it invokes <code>moreau_lower_of_lipschitz</code>, which performs the scalar square completion using <code>hK</code>, and rearranges the resulting inequality. The assumptions retained from the section are convexity <code>hf</code>, a global lower bound <code>hb</code> and positive scale <code>hlam</code>. The conclusion bounds a function-value difference; marginal and center comparisons are separate subsequent applications.</p>', 'center-stability': '<p><code>marginal_center_distance</code> assumes the uniform two-sided potential comparison as <code>hgap</code>, continuity of the original potential as <code>hU</code>, and differentiable strong convexity of the comparison potential as <code>hV</code> and <code>hsc</code>. <code>hmaxU</code> and <code>hmaxV</code> specify both marginal centers. It calls <code>marginal_center_distance_sq</code> for the quadratic-growth argument, deduces nonnegativity of the radicand in <code>hnonneg</code>, and uses <code>Real.le_sqrt</code> to obtain the norm estimate. Substitution of the previously proved Moreau gap gives the smoothing-scale expression without differentiating the nonsmooth potential.</p>', 'smoothed-particle-bound': '<p>In <code>moreau_myipla_parameter_rms_original</code>, <code>ρ</code> is the actual MYIPLA output law. <code>moreau_myipla_isProbability</code> and <code>moreau_myipla_parameter_memLp</code> first provide normalization and the finite projected moment. <code>hmove</code>, from <code>sqrt_integral_center_le</code>, is Minkowski for moving the center. <code>hdist</code> comes from <code>moreau_marginal_center_distance</code>; <code>hbound</code> is the proved smoothed-center theorem <code>moreau_myipla_parameter_rms</code>. After reversing the center-distance norm, the final arithmetic adds those two bounds. The hypotheses retain the lower bound <code>hb</code>, value Lipschitzness <code>hK</code>, both marginal-maximizer premises and Gaussian independence <code>hGaussian</code>.</p>', 'splitting-energy': "<p><code>splittingCouplingForcing_le</code> is the scalar estimate used after the stationary coupling has supplied its four forcing terms. <code>hlocal</code> bounds the squared increment by twice the sum of squares. <code>hGscale</code> uses <code>hsmall</code> to remove one time power, and <code>hlocal'</code> collects that estimate. <code>hweight</code> allows multiplication by the Young weight. The identity <code>heq</code> and bound <code>htwice</code> control its coefficient; <code>hdelta'</code> inserts the potential gap. Finally <code>hC</code> expands the proposed constant and <code>hfirst</code> bounds the weighted smooth-error term. Unfolding <code>splittingCouplingForcing</code> leaves precisely the scalar inequality stated above. The actual law recurrence is established separately in the splitting endpoint module.</p>", 'proximal-parameter': '<p><code>pipgla_parameter_rms_prox_comparison</code> constructs the two square-integrable chains <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> on the same probability space and the comparison radius <code>c</code>. <code>hpoint</code> uses <code>pipglaChain_parameter_comparison_bound</code> at common initial input, and <code>hXY</code> integrates the resulting pointwise bound. The centered projected vectors <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> satisfy <code>hAB</code>; projection contraction and the triangle inequality give <code>hn</code>. <code>hnorm</code> identifies each law-based root integral with its corresponding Hilbert-space norm using <code>pipglaLaws_eq_lpLaw</code>, <code>integral_map</code> and <code>lp_norm_sq_eq_integral</code>. Both proximal scales are positive; this comparison does not require the noises themselves to be independent Gaussian vectors.</p>', 'noise-removal': '<p><code>mypgd_myipla_actual_transport_sq</code> first uses <code>memLp_map_measure_iff</code> and <code>Lp.memLp initial</code> to transfer square integrability to the initial distribution. It applies <code>mypgd_myipla_markov_transport_sq</code>, the transition-law coupling theorem containing the centered-noise cancellation and geometric recurrence. The two rewrites <code>mypgdLaws_eq_markovLaws</code> and <code>myiplaLaws_eq_markovLaws</code> identify these transition iterates with the actual algorithms under <code>hGaussian</code>. Its conclusion includes finite transport before the ordinary real squared-distance bound. The following declaration <code>mypgd_parameter_rms_le_myipla</code> applies projection to that finite bound.</p>'}
_ARGUMENT_DETAILS = {'quadratic-models': '<p>To check the contraction algebra, put \\(c=(m+L)/2\\) and \\(r=(L-m)/2\\). The completed-square inequality says \\(\\|v-cd\\|\\le r\\|d\\|\\). Since \\(d-hv=(1-hc)d-h(v-cd)\\), the triangle inequality gives \\(\\|d-hv\\|\\le(|1-hc|+hr)\\|d\\|\\) for \\(h\\ge0\\). If \\(hc\\le1\\), the coefficient is \\(1-hc+hr=1-mh\\); if \\(hc\\ge1\\), it is \\(hc-1+hr=Lh-1\\). These equal the larger of \\(|1-mh|\\) and \\(|1-Lh|\\). In the later strictly stable regimes that coefficient is below one. The deterministic comparison itself only needs the stated nonnegative step and \\(m\\le L\\), \\(m+L>0\\). \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'gibbs-moments': "<p>For the second-moment passage, let \\(S\\) be a directional score and apply its exponential bound at \\(t\\) and \\(-t\\). Their average gives \\(\\mathbb E\\cosh(tS)\\le e^{Lt^2/2}\\). The nonnegative functions \\(2(\\cosh(tS)-1)/t^2\\) converge to \\(S^2\\) as \\(t\\to0\\). Fatou's lemma therefore gives \\(\\mathbb ES^2\\le\\liminf_{t\\to0}2(e^{Lt^2/2}-1)/t^2=L\\). This obtains integrability and the bound together, without assuming an integrable derivative of the moment-generating function in advance. Finite-dimensional orthonormal expansion gives \\(\\|g\\|^2=\\sum_{i=1}^d\\langle g,e_i\\rangle^2\\); integrating that finite sum gives \\(Ld\\), also when the index set is empty. \\(\\square\\)</p>", 'stationary-reference': '<p>The last law-identification step can be checked directly. For a frequency \\(v\\), the inequality \\(|e^{ia}-e^{ib}|\\le|a-b|\\) gives a bound \\(\\|v\\|\\mathbb E\\|X_n^{\\mathrm{Euler}}-X_n^{\\mathrm{adjusted}}\\|\\) for the difference of characteristic functions. Mean-square convergence of the Euler endpoint also gives convergence of its characteristic function, since Cauchy–Schwarz bounds its mean absolute error by its root mean square error. Both errors tend to zero, while the adjusted characteristic function is always that of Gibbs. Hence the limiting integral solution has the Gibbs characteristic function at every frequency, and uniqueness of characteristic functions identifies its law. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'local-defect': '<p>The scalar integrations are \\(\\int_0^hs\\,ds=h^2/2\\) and \\(\\int_0^h\\sqrt s\\,ds=2h^{3/2}/3\\). For \\(h>0\\), \\(L>0\\) and \\(d>0\\), factoring \\(Lh\\sqrt{hd}\\) from the resulting bound leaves \\(\\sqrt{Lh}/2+2\\sqrt2/3\\le7\\sqrt2/6\\). Its square is \\(49/18\\), whereas \\((33/20)^2=1089/400\\); cross multiplication gives \\(19600\\le19602\\), proving the numerical comparison. If a factor vanishes, the unfactored inequality proves the result directly, so no division by zero enters the estimate. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'synchronous-coupling': '<p>The use of Minkowski is \\(\\|A+B\\|_{L^2}\\le\\|A\\|_{L^2}+\\|B\\|_{L^2}\\) with \\(A=T_h(X_k)-T_h(Y_{kh})\\) and \\(B=R_{k,h}\\). The pointwise contraction implies \\(\\mathbb E\\|A\\|^2\\le q^2\\mathbb E\\|X_k-Y_{kh}\\|^2\\), so taking nonnegative square roots gives the factor \\(q\\). The joint law is a coupling because its coordinate pushforwards are precisely the numerical and reference laws. By definition, \\(W_2\\) is the infimum of \\((\\int\\|x-y\\|^2d\\lambda)^{1/2}\\) over such couplings; it is therefore at most this particular root mean square error. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'geometric-sum': '<p>The induction begins with \\(a_0\\le a_0\\), since the empty sum is zero. Multiplying its induction hypothesis by \\(q\\ge0\\) and using the one-step inequality gives \\(a_{k+1}\\le q^{k+1}a_0+b(1+q+\\cdots+q^k)\\). For the infimum step, choose for every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) an initial coupling whose root cost is at most \\(W_2(\\mu_0,\\pi)+\\varepsilon\\). Apply the same output bound and let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\). This is legitimate because the numerical output law is independent of the auxiliary coupling and \\(q^k\\ge0\\). Finite initial transport and the target second moment supply the finite-cost couplings used here. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'scaled-geometry': "<p>Write \\(w=z-z'\\) and let \\(v\\) be an arbitrary direction. The chain rule gives \\(\\langle\\nabla V_N(z)-\\nabla V_N(z'),v\\rangle=\\sum_i\\langle\\nabla U(J_i z)-\\nabla U(J_i z'),J_i v\\rangle\\). Cauchy–Schwarz in each pair and then in the finite particle sum gives an upper bound \\(L(\\sum_i\\|J_iw\\|^2)^{1/2}(\\sum_i\\|J_iv\\|^2)^{1/2}=NL\\|w\\|\\|v\\|\\). Set \\(v=\\nabla V_N(z)-\\nabla V_N(z')\\); if it is nonzero, divide by its positive norm, and otherwise the desired estimate is zero. This proves the gradient bound without estimating the operator norms of the individual pair maps separately. \\(\\square\\)</p>", 'algorithm-law': '<p>In a latent block the scaled drift is \\(-(\\gamma/N)\\sqrt N\\nabla_xU\\). Multiplication by \\(\\sqrt N\\) gives \\(-\\gamma\\nabla_xU\\), because \\((\\sqrt N)^2=N\\). Its noise coefficient becomes \\(\\sqrt N\\sqrt{2\\gamma/N}=\\sqrt{2\\gamma}\\). In the parameter block no coordinate rescaling is needed. Since the current state is a measurable function of initialization and the preceding innovations, independence of the entire Gaussian sequence and initialization makes the next innovation independent of that state. Consequently conditioning on the current state gives the stated Gaussian transition, and induction identifies its iterates with the algorithmic laws. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'parameter-target': "<p>More explicitly, for \\(a,b\\ge0\\), \\(a+b=1\\), joint strong convexity gives \\(U(a\\theta+b\\theta',ax+bx')\\le aU(\\theta,x)+bU(\\theta',x')-abm(\\|\\theta-\\theta'\\|^2+\\|x-x'\\|^2)/2\\). Discard the nonpositive latent correction and exponentiate the reversed inequality. Prékopa–Leindler then gives \\(\\ell(a\\theta+b\\theta')\\ge e^{abm\\|\\theta-\\theta'\\|^2/2}\\ell(\\theta)^a\\ell(\\theta')^b\\). All marginal integrals are positive and finite by the Gaussian bounds. Taking negative logarithms gives the defining strong-convexity inequality for \\(F=-\\log\\ell\\). This is a statement about integration over latent variables, so it does not identify the marginal maximizer with a joint minimizer. \\(\\square\\)</p>", 'equilibrium-radius': '<p>The lifted Gibbs law projects to the ordinary Gibbs law with potential \\(NF\\): the change-of-variable factor in the latent integral cancels with the partition factor. Strong convexity scales from \\(m\\) to \\(Nm\\), while the parameter dimension remains \\(d_\\theta\\). Apply the continuous-potential concentration estimate in that space and then take the nonnegative square root. Integrability follows first from the strongly convex lifted potential and the bounded parameter projection; thus the root integral is an ordinary finite number. Neither a Lipschitz constant for the marginal gradient nor independence of the particles is used in this concentration step. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'transport-projection': '<p>For a nonnegative random variable \\(R\\), write \\(\\|R\\|_{L^2}=(\\mathbb ER^2)^{1/2}\\). The triangle inequality in this norm follows by expanding the square and applying Cauchy–Schwarz to the mixed product. With the displayed coupling, it bounds the parameter error by the coupling root cost plus the equilibrium root moment. That equilibrium moment depends only on the second marginal, so it is the same for every admissible coupling. Taking the infimum over all finite-cost couplings therefore leaves it unchanged and gives the claimed transport projection bound. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'particle-scaling': '<p>The dimension rescaling is \\(sD=(\\gamma/N)(d_\\theta+Nd_x)=\\gamma(d_x+d_\\theta/N)\\). Because \\(N>0\\), the ratio of the lifted smoothness and curvature is \\((NL)/(Nm)=L/m\\), and the small-step condition \\(\\gamma/N\\le2/(N(m+L))\\) is equivalent to \\(\\gamma\\le2/(m+L)\\). These identities give exactly the displayed contraction and numerical error. The initial square-integrable state gives finite initial transport to the square-integrable Gibbs target, and independent Gaussian innovations identify the law to which the estimate applies. If the full state has dimension zero, both dimensions are zero and the parameter error is identically zero. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'proximal-calculus': '<p>For the remainder calculation put \\(e(x)=g_2^\\eta(x)\\) and \\(r(x)=(x-P_\\eta x)/\\eta\\). Evaluation at \\(P_\\eta x\\) gives \\(e(x+u)\\le e(x)+\\langle r(x),u\\rangle+\\|u\\|^2/(2\\eta)\\). Convex support at \\(P_\\eta x\\), followed by minimization over the competitor for the envelope at \\(x+u\\), gives \\(e(x+u)\\ge e(x)+\\langle r(x),u\\rangle\\). Hence the remainder after subtracting the linear functional lies between zero and \\(\\|u\\|^2/(2\\eta)\\). Division by \\(\\|u\\|\\) makes it tend to zero, which is the definition of differentiability with derivative \\(u\\mapsto\\langle r(x),u\\rangle\\). Residual nonexpansiveness gives \\(\\|r(x)-r(y)\\|\\le\\eta^{-1}\\|x-y\\|\\), so this derivative is continuous. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'finite-smoothing-gap': '<p>The square completion is \\(-Kr+r^2/(2\\eta)=(r-\\eta K)^2/(2\\eta)-\\eta K^2/2\\), valid for \\(\\eta>0\\). The two pointwise potential bounds imply \\(e^{-\\delta_\\eta}e^{-V_\\eta}\\le e^{-U}\\le e^{-V_\\eta}\\). Their latent integrals obey the same inequalities. Since these integrals are positive, applying the increasing logarithm and then changing sign gives \\(F_\\eta\\le F_U\\le F_\\eta+\\delta_\\eta\\) with precisely the same gap. A parameter-independent Jacobian from scaled coordinates cancels before this comparison is used for the marginal centers. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'center-stability': "<p>The quadratic-growth inequality follows even without a derivative of the marginal: apply strong convexity to \\((1-t)\\theta_\\eta+t\\theta_U\\), use minimality at \\(\\theta_\\eta\\), divide by \\(t>0\\), and let \\(t\\downarrow0\\). This gives \\(m\\|\\theta_U-\\theta_\\eta\\|^2/2\\le F_\\eta(\\theta_U)-F_\\eta(\\theta_\\eta)\\). The upper bound by \\(\\delta_\\eta\\) then yields \\(\\|\\theta_U-\\theta_\\eta\\|^2\\le2\\delta_\\eta/m=\\eta K^2/m\\). Nonnegative square roots give the stated expression, including \\(K=0\\). The hypotheses specify both centers as marginal maximizers; the comparison does not assume uniqueness of the original nonsmooth model's center. \\(\\square\\)</p>", 'smoothed-particle-bound': '<p>At the final projection step use \\(\\theta-\\theta_U=(\\theta-\\theta_\\eta)+(\\theta_\\eta-\\theta_U)\\) under the MYIPLA output probability. Minkowski bounds its root second moment by the smoothed-center root second moment plus the constant center displacement. The square-integrability of the projected output is established from the finite initial second moment and Lipschitz updates before taking this root. Substituting the sharp concentration, the transport theorem and the center comparison yields the four displayed terms. Every scale is positive and the step must satisfy the smoothness-dependent restriction with \\(L_\\eta=L_1+\\eta^{-1}\\); a small smoothing scale cannot be chosen independently of that step. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'splitting-energy': '<p>For the scalar absorption, put \\(R=Gs+\\sigma\\sqrt s\\). Since \\((Gs-\\sigma\\sqrt s)^2\\ge0\\), \\(R^2\\le2G^2s^2+2\\sigma^2s\\). The restriction \\(s\\le1/a\\) makes this at most \\(2s(G^2/a+\\sigma^2)\\). Consequently \\((1+(as)^{-1})b^2s^2R^2\\le2b^2s^2(s+1/a)(G^2/a+\\sigma^2)\\le4b^2s^2(G^2/a+\\sigma^2)/a\\). The gap contribution is \\(2s\\delta\\le s^2c^2\\). Adding the other two terms gives \\(\\mathcal F\\le4b^2s^2(G^2/a+\\sigma^2)/a+2s^2Gc+2s^2c^2=as^2C_N\\). Dividing the constant forcing in the squared-error recurrence by its gap \\(as\\) leaves \\(sC_N\\). \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'proximal-parameter': '<p>Let \\(d_k\\) be the state distance between the two common-noise chains, with \\(d_0=0\\). One update gives \\(d_{k+1}\\le qd_k+K|\\lambda-\\eta|\\), because the same-scale map contracts by \\(q\\) and the change of scale contributes the second term. Induction gives \\(d_k\\le K|\\lambda-\\eta|\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}q^j\\le K|\\lambda-\\eta|/(m\\gamma)\\). This is pointwise for every shared noise sequence. Parameter projection has norm at most one; taking the root second moment and using Minkowski yields the same additive constant for the output root mean square errors about any fixed center. Independence of the noise is not required for this comparison itself. \\(\\square\\)</p>', 'noise-removal': '<p>Write the full Gaussian innovation as independent parameter and latent blocks. Conditional on both current states, the deterministic update discrepancy and shared latent innovation have zero cross expectation with the centered parameter innovation. Expanding the squared norm therefore adds only \\(2\\gamma d_\\theta/N\\). The denominator in the sum is \\(1-q^2=m\\gamma(2-m\\gamma)\\), where \\(q=1-m\\gamma\\). The admissible step gives \\(0\\le m\\gamma\\le1\\), so \\(2-m\\gamma\\ge1\\), and division by this positive gap gives \\(E_n\\le2d_\\theta/(Nm)\\). Taking nonnegative square roots and applying the parameter transport projection proves the stated comparison radius. \\(\\square\\)</p>'}
for _argument in (ULA_PROOF, IPLA_PROOF, PROXIMAL_PROOF,):
    for _part in _argument:
        _part["lean"] = _ARGUMENT_LEAN[_part["id"]]
        _part["body"] += _ARGUMENT_DETAILS[_part["id"]]
